+## 6. Statistical methods / specifications
 
-**Dynamic specification** (Equation 8) replaces $Y_{it}$ with $Y_{it}^{+} = \sum_{s=0}^{2} Y_{i,t+s}$ (rolling 3-day sum) to capture lagged effects. A variant drops alert days whose previous day was also alerted to avoid double-counting in policy simulations.
+**First-stage regression (Eq. 5)**:
 
-**Theoretical framework** (Section II): individual utility $U_i(a_i, pm) = b_i(a_i) - s_i^{pvt} p_s(a_i, pm)$ with private cost-of-sickness $s_i^{pvt}$ and probability of sickness $p_s$. Beliefs about PM depend on whether the running variable is below or above $c$: $pm_i = pm^{avg}$ if below, $pm^{hi}$ if above, with $pm^{hi} \approx c$. Revealed preference gives $\Delta U_i \geq 0$. Social welfare $W_i = U_i + E_i$; $\Delta W_i = \Delta U_i + \Delta E_i$; so $\Delta E_i$ is a lower bound on $\Delta W_i$. **The key empirical move is that the publicly-subsidized share of healthcare expenditures (~70%) measures $\Delta E_i$.**
+$$
+\text{Alert}_{it} = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \mathbf{1}[\widetilde{PM}_{it} \geq 0] + \gamma_2 \widetilde{PM}_{it} + \gamma_3 \widetilde{PM}_{it} \cdot \mathbf{1}[\widetilde{PM}_{it} \geq 0] + X_{1it}' \lambda + X_{2t}' \mu + \delta_i + \varepsilon_{it}
+$$
 
